  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Another cold night in store for central PA.\n\nHigh pressure will slide east overnight, with winds shifting \naround to the SW across west central PA. This should make frost \nless likely tonight in the places that were coldest this \nmorning. However, temperatures will be cool enough to support\npatchy fog for the northern tier and Susquehanna valley. \n\nA frost advisory is in place for the portions of Eastern PA in\nthe Middle Susquehanna Valley where conditions are expected to\nline up the best for frost. The northern tier will likely be\ncool enough for frost but an approaching upper level wave will\ninduce some southerly surface winds ahead of a cold front \novernight. These winds will likely preclude widespread frost \nformation. The Lower Susquehanna River Valley will likely be too\nwarm for widespread frost formation. Both areas though will \nlikely see some degree of patchy frost, particularly in low \nlying valleys.\n\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2: Extended stretch of dry and seasonal Fall \nweather this week, followed by a chance of rain on Sunday\ninto Monday.\n\nHigh confidence in a prolonged stretch of dry and seasonable \nFall weather this week. Expect cool nights and refreshing \nafternoons with low humidity through Saturday. A tropical \ndisturbance in the gulf will move up north towards the Ohio \nRiver Valley this weekend bringing tropical moisture and rain \npotential Sunday afternoon into Monday. This system looks fairly\nprogressive as it begins to get absorbed into some strong \nwesterly flow in Canada. However, robust forcing and PWATs in \nthe 1.5-1.75\" range, particularly in the southern tier of the \nstate, may yield some periods of heavy rain. Uncertainty \nremains regarding how this disturbance plays out.",
